{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"dceb290501b8cbd1ce2c06985435f7832cc845e41f8197d0bc06effe6a327b54","slug":"index-11","location":"/chapters/ch11_audio_features/0_index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"11 - Audio Features","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"0_index.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/0_index-a4004234f8a5d3ddd64e2affa842085f.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A radio station keeps every broadcast. One hour of its morning show, sampled 48,000 times per second, is a list of 172.8 million amplitude values per channel, and the archive grows by 24 such hours every day. The people who work with the archive ask very different questions. The archivist wants to mark where the talk ends and the music begins. The station must report every song it played for royalties, even when the presenter talks over the intro. A journalist looks for the interview in which a guest mentioned a local politician by name, and the guest spoke Swiss German. A music editor wants upbeat songs at about 120 beats per minute for a new playlist. No comparison of amplitude values answers any of these questions.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"rc9kwUIxL0"}],"key":"wM3gBPeOAo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each question needs its own description of the sound, and almost all of them start from the same picture: the spectrogram, which shows which frequencies are present at which moment. A long recording is described piece by piece, much as a long text is split into passages: in short frames of a few tens of milliseconds and in segments of a few seconds, the retrieval units of sound. This chapter computes what goes into these units. The results come in four kinds, and each one feeds a tool from earlier chapters: feature vectors for similarity search, labels such as “speech” and “music” for facets and filters, transcripts for the text index, and fingerprints for a hash lookup that names one exact recording, even from a noisy phone recording (","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"zbjIFVhUR8"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Time-Based Media and Specialized Solutions","key":"n1rc3XhQmB"}],"identifier":"content-analysis-time-specialized","label":"content-analysis-time-specialized","url":"/time-and-specialized-solutions","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/time-and-specialized-solutions.json","key":"UgrCPhzE0S"},{"type":"text","value":").","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"VqnB0p4kIK"}],"key":"cMCn8vrDaJ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For most of these results, two generations of methods exist side by side. The first one is designed by hand from signal processing and from the way the ear works. The mel scale follows the frequency resolution of the ear, and MFCCs, a short vector of coefficients on that scale, served speech and music analysis for decades. A small decision tree on a few such features separates speech from music. The second generation learns its features from large audio collections. Pretrained models label sounds, CLAP answers a text query such as “applause” without a classifier trained for that class, and Whisper and recognizers built on large language models transcribe speech in many languages. The second generation has not made the first obsolete. Recognizers still miss the rare names and dialect words that users search for most, and the songs for the royalty report are still identified by a fingerprint: a compact code built from the strongest peaks of the spectrogram, which finds one recording among millions within a second.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"Aw0774nkD2"}],"key":"CC6W4GTKJD"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"tip","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What you’ll learn","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"eqj8v9rgR1"}],"key":"RUEttrRfm6"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how sampling and the short-time Fourier transform turn a recording into a spectrogram, and choose a frame size from the trade-off between time and frequency resolution.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"MTYjLIki3A"}],"key":"T4IQySN8O9"}],"key":"k7pCjyEajy"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compute perceptual features such as short-time energy, spectral centroid, and MFCCs, and musical features such as chroma and tempo, and explain why MFCCs use the mel scale.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"icoysm3Z5z"}],"key":"URgvw7HdKY"}],"key":"YUbHNJrztd"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare a hand-crafted speech/music classifier with pretrained audio models and CLAP for labeling audio and for searching it with text.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"BFaYrN1y4g"}],"key":"GVM1sabcgX"}],"key":"mYLW1ou8ql"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how speech recognition developed from phoneme HMMs to Whisper and LLM-based recognizers, and evaluate transcripts across languages and dialects with word and character error rates.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"tollXH6qfD"}],"key":"zu0bzdNPSD"}],"key":"Fbpk2T9RH5"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how audio fingerprinting identifies one recording from a noisy clip with a constellation map, combinatorial hashing, and an offset histogram.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"ys7yMubpP2"}],"key":"owg4x3APam"}],"key":"mc5XVjnJjq"}],"key":"MJ4rg9kQbj"}],"key":"TVc64Uks2M"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"seealso","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prerequisites","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"yFgiopts8M"}],"key":"xiYXmYdxjk"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Frames and segments as the retrieval units of audio, and the problem of naming a song from a noisy recording: ","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"aLMtb7FAco"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Time-Based Media and Specialized Solutions","key":"BwGoEIXjJH"}],"identifier":"content-analysis-time-specialized","label":"content-analysis-time-specialized","url":"/time-and-specialized-solutions","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/time-and-specialized-solutions.json","key":"AQxO6DlFNY"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"rkcvkjkNjl"}],"key":"ziZ8y949NB"}],"key":"YcEhysVuic"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Perceptual features, classifiers, and speech versus music as the classic example: ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"lEuuK85OWN"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automated Feature Extraction","key":"UBPu4FUjl1"}],"identifier":"content-analysis-feature-extraction","label":"content-analysis-feature-extraction","url":"/automated-feature-extraction","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/automated-feature-extraction.json","key":"LMa7F3fO3I"},{"type":"text","value":". Evaluating classifiers: ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"pXc50jzmg8"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evaluating Text Classifiers","key":"uqpIuwuWC2"}],"identifier":"performance-evaluation-text-classifiers","label":"performance-evaluation-text-classifiers","url":"/evaluating-text-classifiers","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/evaluating-text-classifiers.json","key":"EaIwVGwd4o"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"Dtm12NLu0v"}],"key":"OnmPCWpFv3"}],"key":"CGM2Wx2FBu"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{